{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"blender uv loop data blender uv loop data","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md","fileName":"Blender UV Loop Data.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Blender UV Loop Data","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 09:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Automated Blender Texturing and Rendering]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Automated Blender Texturing and Rendering","blenderpythonapi.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"blender uv loop data blender uv loop data","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md","fileName":"Blender UV Loop Data.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Blender UV Loop Data","heading":"Blender UV Loop Data","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Blender stores UV coordinates on loop data associated with face corners rather than only on unique mesh vertices. For a given face and corner, the loop's UV value corresponds to that face's vertex coordinate, creating a path between topology and texture placement.\n\nSeveral face corners can occupy the same point in 3D space while holding different UV coordinates. This is necessary at texture seams, where one geometric vertex appears in separate parts of the flattened image. Algorithms must therefore identify the face corner, not just the spatial vertex, when assigning UVs.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Automated Blender Texturing and Rendering","blenderpythonapi.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender UV Loop Data.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blen